# Staging — copy to `.env.stage` and fill in. Used only by `pnpm seed:stage`, which seeds staging
# *from your machine* over the network.
#
# NOT the file to use when you are already on the staging host. Postgres runs on the same instance
# there, so `DB_HOST` is `localhost`, and `pnpm seed:stage` refuses a local host by design (see
# below). On the box you run `pnpm seed` with `SEED_PASSWORD` set for that one command, against the
# environment file the deploy already wrote.
#
# `.env.stage` is gitignored. This file is the template, and it is versioned so that the two
# variables the seed refuses to run without are impossible to miss.
#
# What `pnpm seed:stage` does: TRUNCATE every table in the database below, then write the same
# fixture `pnpm seed` writes locally — two locations, nine groups spread over Monday to Saturday,
# families, attendance, invoices, payments, leads, announcements. It is not additive and there is no
# undo. Anything a tester typed into staging is gone.

# --- The database it is allowed to wipe -----------------------------------------------------
#
# Fill DB_HOST in. `dotenv -e .env.stage` does NOT fail when this file is missing or a line is
# commented out — it loads nothing, and the app then falls back to `localhost`. `pnpm seed:stage`
# refuses a local host for exactly that reason, so a half-filled file stops the command rather than
# quietly wiping your own development database.

DB_HOST=db.stage.example.com
DB_PORT=5432
DB_USER=itbridge
DB_PASSWORD=
DB_NAME=itbridge_stage

# Must equal DB_NAME above, spelled out rather than a yes/no flag.
#
# This variable will live in this file permanently, and a permanent "yes" authorises whatever
# DB_NAME happens to say next month — a production database reached by a typo included. Naming the
# database means the grant can only ever authorise the one you meant.
SEED_ALLOW_NON_LOCAL=itbridge_stage

# The password every seeded account gets, admin included. No default: locally the seed uses
# `parola123`, which is written in this repository and in the README, and staging is reachable by
# anyone who knows the hostname. Pick something, put it in your password manager, and note that the
# seed deliberately does NOT print it back — it would end up in whatever captured the run's output.
SEED_PASSWORD=

# --- The rest of what the API needs to boot -------------------------------------------------
#
# The seed imports the real DataSource, which imports `load-env.ts`, which runs
# `apps/api/src/config/env.validation.ts` — unlike the migration CLI, the seed does not set
# SKIP_ENV_VALIDATION. So the JWT secrets must be present, at least 16 characters, and different
# from each other. These are the names the validator reads; JWT_SECRET and JWT_REFRESH_SECRET are
# not read by anything.

JWT_ACCESS_TOKEN_SECRET=
JWT_REFRESH_TOKEN_SECRET=
AWS_REGION=eu-north-1

# Optional. Pins the seeded school to a fixed day instead of today — useful when two runs have to
# produce identical data. Left unset, staging always opens on a school that has a class today.
# SEED_TODAY=2026-03-16
